# ESPHome for esp-component.yml's esphome job (esphome config, validating the
# ESPHome external component against esphome/tests/ac3forge-test.yaml).
# Regenerate the lock after any change here with:
#   python -m piptools compile --generate-hashes --strip-extras \
#     --output-file=requirements-esphome.txt requirements-esphome.in
esphome

# Not imported directly - platformio (esphome's own embedded-build
# dependency) pulls in starlette, which carries five disclosed CVEs at every
# version this graph can resolve (two high: an SSRF/NTLM-credential-theft
# path via StaticFiles on Windows, and a request.form() size-limit bypass
# enabling DoS). Confirmed NOT fixable by pinning forward: esphome==2026.9.0
# (latest) hard-pins platformio==6.1.19 exactly, which caps
# starlette<0.53,>=0.19, and 0.52.1 - already the version an unpinned
# resolve picks - is the newest release that cap allows. Starlette itself
# has long since moved past that cap (1.3.1 is the first release with all
# five CVEs fixed), and platformio's own cap has separately been raised too
# - platformio 6.2.0 allows starlette<2 - but esphome has not adopted that
# newer platformio yet, so the resolver still rejects any starlette bump
# (verified via `pip install --dry-run`: ResolutionImpossible). Explicit
# here (not left to resolve implicitly) so this reads as a confirmed,
# accepted limitation rather than an oversight: this job only ever runs
# `esphome config` to validate YAML, never platformio's local dashboard or
# remote-agent server, so the actual vulnerable code paths (StaticFiles,
# request.form() against attacker input) are present in the environment but
# never exercised. Revisit once esphome bumps its platformio pin past 6.2.0.
starlette==0.52.1
